// @error field 'x' expects a value, got void
// CONTRACT (must-error): an object literal's field slot demands a value, and a void call has
// none. The field type of an UN-annotated literal comes from the initializer itself, so no
// assignability rule sees it; the shape then carried a `void` member into the emitter, which
// refused it as `emitProgram: ref valtype with no interned shape` — an internal name for a
// heap type, naming nothing the author wrote.
//
// The refusal names the FIELD, and the field is poisoned to the error type once reported, so
// an annotated literal (`const p: Pt = { x: pv() }`) keeps its single "cannot assign
// {x: void} to 'p' of type {x: i32}" rather than gaining a second message for one mistake.
//
// A VERDICT pin: the program does not build either way, and the fix moves the refusal from
// the emit tier to the check tier — a change in channel and message, which is what a verdict
// pin expresses and a `@log` cannot.
function pv() { print(1) }
const p = { x: pv() }
print(p.x)
